 {"id": "AUG-090", "title": "Substrate isolation under-documented: DUCKBRAIN_DATA_DIR / DUCKBRAIN_NAMESPACES_PATH alone do not isolate a second substrate from a co-located production DuckBrain", "status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "complexity": 3, "capability_tags": ["docs", "substrate"], "created_at": "2026-09-28T23:04:59+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T23:04:59+00:00", "reasoning": "Review lane 2026-09-28 (carterlasalle, commit f3c365e) hit this live. The README Substrate pin says 'give the daemon a data directory' (DUCKBRAIN_DATA_DIR=~/dd-data), but on a box already running production DuckBrain (:3000), starting a second instance with DUCKBRAIN_DATA_DIR + DUCKBRAIN_NAMESPACES_PATH pointed at a scratch dir STILL lists ~30 production namespaces from /api/namespaces. Root cause: the namespace registry (duckbrain.config.json namespaceMappings) is a shared surface; the census is the union of config namespaceMappings + the on-disk scan, so DUCKBRAIN_NAMESPACES_PATH alone does not override it. Fix that worked: also set DUCKBRAIN_CONFIG_PATH to a config with namespaceMappings={} and namespacesPath=<scratch>. auger writes themselves were still isolated (correct target dir), but the namespace LIST is wrong, which can mislead an operator into thinking their scratch writes landed in production. Action: document the DUCKBRAIN_CONFIG_PATH escape hatch in the README Substrate pin (or smoke.sh/gate.sh isolation note), or have init warn when the namespace list is non-empty for a fresh namespace.", "review_notes": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "8f212690053aedf62c900e2a6cc2f87d60f03092860de32872cc51d82b8a5bdc"}, "worker_status": "pending", "completed_at": null, "commit_hash": null, "files_changed": [], "lines_added": 0, "lines_removed": 0, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
